## Proof

**Setup and definitions.** Let $S$ be a finite set of $n$ points in $\mathbb{R}^2$ in general position (no four cocircular, no three collinear), so the Delaunay triangulation $D(S)$ is well-defined and unique. The alpha complex filtration is indexed by a parameter $\alpha \ge 0$. Every simplex $\sigma$ of $D(S)$ has an **alpha value** $\alpha(\sigma) \ge 0$, the smallest $\alpha$ at which $\sigma$ enters the complex:

- Every **vertex** has $\alpha(v) = 0$. Since the problem states $S = A_1$, all vertices are already present at the first stage; the nontrivial filtration steps concern edges and triangles.
- For a **Delaunay edge** $e = \{p,q\}$: $\alpha(e)$ is the radius of the smallest closed disk having $p$ and $q$ on its boundary with empty interior (no point of $S$ inside). This equals $|pq|/2$ when $e$ is a Gabriel edge (the diametral disk is empty), and otherwise equals the distance from $p$ to the nearer Voronoi vertex on the Voronoi edge shared by the cells of $p$ and $q$.
- For a **Delaunay triangle** $f = \{p,q,r\}$: $\alpha(f)$ is the circumradius of the triangle $pqr$.

The filtration $S = A_1 \subset A_2 \subset \cdots \subset A_k$ is obtained by sorting all non-vertex simplices by nondecreasing alpha value and inserting them in that order (vertices are all present from the start).

**Claim.** $A_i$ and $A_{i+1}$ differ by exactly one simplex for every $1 \le i \le k-1$ **if and only if** all non-vertex simplices of $D(S)$ have pairwise distinct alpha values:
$$\alpha(\sigma_1) \ne \alpha(\sigma_2) \quad \text{for all distinct simplices } \sigma_1, \sigma_2 \in D(S) \setminus S.$$

**Proof of "if".** If all alpha values are distinct, the sorted insertion order is a strict total order. Each step of the filtration adds exactly the next simplex in this order, so $|A_{i+1} \setminus A_i| = 1$ for every $i$.

**Proof of "only if".** Suppose two distinct simplices $\sigma_1, \sigma_2$ satisfy $\alpha(\sigma_1) = \alpha(\sigma_2) = \alpha^*$. Then both enter at the same threshold $\alpha^*$. In the discrete filtration they appear in the same step, so that step adds at least two simplices, violating the single-simplex condition. (Reindexing the filtration to split them would contradict the definition of the alpha filtration, which groups simplices by their common alpha value.)

**Genericity.** The condition is a **genericity** (general-position) condition on $S$. Each alpha value $\alpha(\sigma)$ is a real-analytic function of the point coordinates (circumradii and edge-length-based quantities are algebraic/analytic functions of coordinates). For any pair of distinct simplices $\sigma_1 \ne \sigma_2$, the equation
$$\alpha(\sigma_1) = \alpha(\sigma_2)$$
defines a proper real-analytic subset (in fact, away from degenerate configurations, a proper algebraic hypersurface) of the configuration space $\mathbb{R}^{2n}$. There are finitely many such pairs, so the locus where *some* pair collides is a finite union of proper analytic subsets, hence has empty interior and Lebesgue measure zero. Its complement — the set of configurations satisfying the distinctness condition — is open and dense of full measure.

**Conclusion.** The desired property holds precisely when the point set $S$ is in **general position with respect to the alpha filtration**, i.e., when no two distinct simplices of the Delaunay triangulation share the same alpha value. This is a generic condition: it holds on an open dense subset of full Lebesgue measure in the configuration space, and it fails exactly on a finite union of proper algebraic/analytic varieties (each given by equating two alpha values).

